x86: emulate AVX2, FMA3, BMI1/2, F16C, MOVBE, GFNI, AVX-VNNI and SHA-NI in #UD

On x86-64 CPUs that implement AVX but lack some of these extensions (Sandy
Bridge, Ivy Bridge, ...), binaries that use them unconditionally die with
SIGILL. Add an optional (CONFIG_X86_AVXEMU, default n) emulator that runs
from handle_invalid_op() for 64-bit user mode:

 - the faulting instruction is fetched with insn_fetch_from_user(), decoded
   and executed by a freestanding scalar-integer core (no SIMD, no libm, no
   libgcc helpers) on a copy of the task GPR and XMM/YMM state;
 - VEX encodings (AVX2, FMA3, BMI1/2, F16C, GFNI, AVX-VNNI) and legacy SSE
   encodings (MOVBE, SHA-NI, GFNI) are handled; legacy 128-bit memory
   operands must be 16-byte aligned (#GP -> SIGSEGV otherwise) and only bits
   127:0 of the destination are written;
 - memory operands go through copy_{from,to}_user(); a bad operand address
   raises SIGSEGV with the operand address as si_addr and leaves state
   untouched;
 - results are committed to pt_regs and the task fpstate (marked stale so it
   is reloaded on return to user), then RIP is advanced;
 - anything not recognised, or an extension the CPU actually has, falls
   through to the normal SIGILL path;
 - CPUID is not altered. "avxemu=0" disables it at boot.

Counters are exposed in debugfs (x86/avxemu_{emulated,declined,faulted}).

diff -urN a/arch/x86/Kconfig b/arch/x86/Kconfig
--- a/arch/x86/Kconfig	2026-11-01 15:56:06.124908707 +0000
+++ b/arch/x86/Kconfig	2026-11-01 20:47:32.426574931 +0000
@@ -1758,6 +1758,27 @@
 	  specific cases in protected and virtual-8086 modes. Emulated
 	  results are dummy.
 
+config X86_AVXEMU
+	bool "Emulate AVX2, FMA3, BMI1/2, F16C, MOVBE, GFNI, AVX-VNNI and SHA-NI"
+	depends on X86_64
+	default y
+	help
+	  On x86-64 CPUs that implement AVX but not (all of) AVX2, FMA3, BMI1,
+	  BMI2, F16C, MOVBE, GFNI, AVX-VNNI and SHA-NI (for example Sandy Bridge
+	  and Ivy Bridge), user space that executes those instructions normally
+	  dies with SIGILL.
+	  This option emulates them in the invalid-opcode exception handler,
+	  so such binaries run, slowly (microseconds per instruction).
+
+	  CPUID is not modified: software that checks CPUID keeps using its
+	  fallback code paths. Unsupported instructions still raise SIGILL.
+	  Emulation is skipped entirely on CPUs that have every extension.
+
+	  This adds an instruction decoder that acts on user-controlled bytes
+	  to the kernel; it only ever touches user memory through
+	  copy_{from,to}_user() and the task's saved register state. It can
+	  be disabled at boot with "avxemu=0".
+
 config CC_HAS_IBT
 	# GCC >= 9 and binutils >= 2.29
 	# Retpoline check to work around https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=93654
